# -- end of source
# Fault injection, testing=AssumeUnknownCallsReturnUndefined: Link assumes that the shell's `id` returns undefined.
# So all that it sees stored into the array are integer literals that are not negative: int[0, 2147483647]. The array holds 1, 2, 3 and -7 in int32 storage.
# The read from int32 storage checks nothing about the number, so its type is int32 and nothing narrower. The version that reads a boxed value checks for an int32 and claims nothing narrower.
# In neither version does the range decide `v < 0`, so the output is that of plain Bun. Before the first of these two rules the program printed 0 and continued.
# Where the range is relied on, at an entry check, it is checked: see a-false-range-of-what-a-list-holds-stops-at-the-door-that-believes-it.ir.
# RUN: run-in-shell mechanisms=all
# CHECK: 1
# CHECK-NEXT: went on
# CHECK-NEXT: exited with 0
# RUN(LIE): run-in-shell mechanisms=all testing=AssumeUnknownCallsReturnUndefined
# LIE: 1
# LIE-NEXT: went on
# LIE-NEXT: exited with 0
